mpleted may thirty first  of 2013 we had over a million images  every one of those million images was  reviewed either by myself or one of four  other employees I can tell you that was  a lot of work but it was necessary to  validate the system the overall pass  rate was not eighty percent as we had  hoped it was about 75% we still have  hopes that we can get that up to closer  to eighty percent and minimize the human  labor labor involved in inspecting all  of those fails the important thing was  was we had no false passes there  were no mislabeled specimens that  slipped through and were passed by the  system out of seven hundred and  forty-three thousand total passes among  the mislabeled specimens in the  validation study of which there were 121  what we learned was was that first of  all the rate that we thought we had  which was one to ten thousand based on  the historic data collected by the  specimen processing Department the ratio  was actually a little bit higher one per  8346 not bad still better than those see  AP publications but not as good as what  we had thought based on the data we were  capturing but this is difficult data to  capture because it requires on all sorts  of after the fact investigation requires  clients to call if they have detected an  error that sort of thing so that's why  the tendency is to miss some of your  errors without an automated system like  we're using here so we were actually  quite pleased with this overall  performance level from our specimen  processing department that they only  mislabeled one specimen out of every  8300 out of those 121 mislabeled  specimens 71 were found by the testing  laboratory our editors before testing  but we had to send 46 of those on for  investigation for possible correction  reports and we ended up with 21  corrected reports now not always is a  corrected report required if the tests  are repeated and the results are the  same after the specimens have been  correctly labeled then there's no need  for a corrected report and so there are  different scenarios in which you correct  the report would not be required but we  did end up sending out 21 corrected  reports out of those 121 miss labels one  of the values of the system however is  that we also find specimens which we  call  mismatch they're primarily spelling  errors so for example the client label  has a patient named Kristin kri st en  and our label our laboratories label has  Kristin spelled Kri STI n so te an  or-tis it's the same patient the medical  record numbers the date of birth  collection dating all the other  parameters match it's just that spelling  error if all of those other parameters  match we continue on with testing but if  those other parameters don't match where  our policies are to call the client and  verify that the patient and spelling and  make sure what we report is the correct  spelling as it turns out this system  found 148 of those so-called mismatched  names and employees had only found about  a third of those prior to testing so  that's an additional value of this OCR  system and we were very fortunate to  have the publication on this system  accepted and published in clinical  chemistry in March of this year a couple  of months ago and and it was a great  honor to have that article accompanied  by an editorial by dr. Robin Felder so  the current results on this system  through last week are we've now run 2.87  million images the mislabel ratio has  dropped a bit to one in 7,500 still  better than the published reports from  the College of American Pathologists but  of those 382 miss labels 50 about fifty  four percent 207 were found prior to  testing we also have identified entered  in 19 specimens as mismatched or with  spelling errors  and we're identifying our employees are  finding about twenty eight percent of  those prior to testing as of last week  we've sent 40 corrected reports to  clients now that's not that much bigger  a number than the 21 before it turns out  that in February of 2013 we moved this  system from one automated track to a  different automated track the first  track that the system was on delivered  the specimens to our high volume  automated core laboratory where  specimens are tested non-stop 24 hours a  day seven days a week and they test so  quickly that they don't often catch the  airs and so 17 of the errors were  wildest but the specimens were being  delivered to the core lab and they had a  total of six hundred thirty six thousand  specimens that for which we had  collected images and done OCR analysis  so that error ratio was one corrected  report for every thirty seven thousand  four hundred and sixteen images that we  collected with the system when we moved  that system to a different track the  specimens went to a different laboratory  section that wasn't running their tests  every day of the week it's the  immunology laboratory some of them the  testing as higher volume and is is run  constantly but some of it is lower  volume and some of the tests are run  twice a week or three times a week or  that sort of thing and what that meant  was was that the OCR was actually in  time I should mention that we don't  review these OCR images right on the  spot the review by an employee is the  next day or in the case of weekend  specimens it's on Monday so it's after  the fact but in the case of the  laboratory section immunology  where the testing is not done constantly  often we were able to tell them of this  error before testing was completed our  testing was even started and so the  error rate has dropped significantly the  corrected reports sent to clients has  dropped to a ratio of 14 97,000 just  simply showing what the power of this  system is and that if we can promptly  review those images will in fact  eliminate any corrected reports and  that's that's our ultimate goal we're  going to be building four more of these  systems as shown by these arrows across  here here's our four different tracks  and specimen processing we're going to  build four more of these systems in this  coming year and we will set up for  employees to review those images  promptly and we believe we can drive  corrected reports related to mislabeled  specimens to near zero so what can you  do in your laboratory to improve your  quality I've talked about lost specimens  and Miss and Miss labeled specimens but  let me just give you some ideas that you  can do that don't involve these  sophisticated automation that we have in  our laboratory so reducing lost  specimens is all about tracking even if  you don't have Adam automation you need  to have some mechanisms for tracking the  specimens where they're going and who's  handling them you can use your L is to  track specimens from specimen processing  in our Cerner lis system we have a  status called central collect which is  the status the specimens are in once  they have been ordered in specimen  processing once they're delivered to the  lab sections they're brought to what's  called in lab status in the cerner  system  this requires an extra barcode read in  the labs to verify the receipt of the  specimens but it's a useful tracking  step to have the labs read the barcode  on the specimen once the specimens in  their hands for specimens being  transported to the lab from clinics or  affiliated hospitals I believe you could  use barcodes to create transfer lists so  you'd have a transfer list of 20 or 30  specimens or however many and that whole  sheet transfer list number such-and-such  would have a barcode you just read one  barcode and you're essentially taking  receipt of the transfer of those  specimens from point A to point B you  can require employees to check out the  specimens if you have a centralized  storage system in many laboratories  employees just simply go in the computer  system and find the location of the  stored specimen tray Iraq number  such-and-such row number column number  and so on and they take the specimen  nobody knows who took the specimen we  had that going on in our laboratory for  a while until finally everybody got  frustrated I had said from the beginning  require employees to check specimens out  and no one believed me finally after  several years they took my advice and  and we don't allow employees to get  specimens without entering their ID in  fact it's a bar code based system so  they have to use their employee badge  which has a bar code if you don't have  your badge you can't checkout specimens  mislabeled specimens can be reduced by  using wireless bedside phlebotomy  systems where the wireless system  queries the lis gets the current orders  Prince specimen labels for those orders  you're at the patient bedside and so  that process is initiated by reading the  patient's wristband barcode not that  many labret laboratories or hospitals  are set up with this kind of a system  yet probably in the neighborhood of  twenty percent but that's something that  although expensive is a huge step  towards reducing mislabeled specimens  also I strongly recommend that  laboratories implement the clsi standard  auto 12 a on specimen label formats what  is the clsi standard auto 12 a it was a  standard published three years ago in  which the format for a label as shown on  these two examples specifies patient  name on the top line of the label as our  committee reviewed a lot of different  label formats we found the patient name  anywhere on the label from first line  second line third line fourth line fifth  line above the barcode below the barcode  left justified right justified sometimes  in the middle and sometimes turned 90  degrees to the orientation of the rest  of the label and the orientation of the  tube and so this standard is not  concerned with the barcode that's a  different clsi standard auto to but this  standard is concerned with the human  readable content so patient name and  their in in the standard are specified  font types font sizes and formats so  patient name on the first line unique  secondary identifier usually a medical  record number or financial number is on  the second line left justified right  justified is patient date of birth age  and sex then below the barcode left  justified is the collection date and  time and a phlebotomist ID and the rest  of the region below the barcode is left  for the laboratory to choose for their  optional information and there's a  version of this label that has a  priority code down the left side of the  label so this priority code would be  easily visible if the tubes were in Iraq  or whatever because the standard  requires that these labels be placed so  they're readable as they are now if the  top of the tube the capper the stopper  is on the left end of the is to the left  of the label so in summary experience in  our laboratory over 22 years in  implementing process improvements in  automation has led to a steady reduction  in Los specimens to a level consistently  below 1 per 100,000 build units and some  months in the Six Sigma region in other  words less than point 3 4 per 100,000  also we have new OCR technology for  identifying possible mislabeled  specimens and this technology has the  prospect of perhaps someday achieving  Six Sigma quality levels when we have it  fully implemented on our automation and  I have provided several improvement  suggestions for use in your laboratories  that can offer opportunities to achieve  meaningful reductions in error rates  without some of these expensive  automation systems so thank you and on  this slide is the information about  where you can get continuing education  credits at this point I'm happy to take  questions and I i will look at the  questions that have been submitted and  read them and give you some responses so  the first the first question is did we  investigate errors on PT risk barcode we  have not however that has been published  in clinical chemistry by a group from  Emory University the first author's name  was Snyder synd er I don't have the  citation in front of me but I'm going to  say it was fall of 2011 or 2012 and I'm  sorry I can't be more specific but the  Emery group investigated barcode errors  associated with the the wristband  barcodes and primarily most of the  errors were occurring with different  point of care reading devices so their  overall project they improved the  quality of the wristband barcodes but  then they standardized on some of the  barcode reading devices and added check  digits and a number of other  improvements and it's a very informative  article so that appears to be the only  question that was submitted so with that  I thank you for attending this  conference today I've enjoyed presenting  it to you and I hope you will look at  other bio conference live presentations  thank you very much  you
 

